# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
BLOCKS = {
  "base": [
    ("Slope Has Two Jobs on a Pool Deck, Not Just One",
     "<p>A patio only needs to slope away from the house. A pool deck does that too, but it also has to carry water toward deck drains or a perimeter channel without letting it pool against the coping or run back into the pool itself. We set that two-direction grade into the subgrade before any base goes down, typically shaping a slight fall from the house side out to the drain line rather than one flat pitch across the whole deck. Skip that step on a {city} job and an afternoon storm leaves standing water right where people walk barefoot between the pool and the house, long after the rest of the yard has drained.</p>"),
    ("Base Work Where a Screen Cage Footer Lands on the Deck",
     "<p>Most pool decks in this market get enclosed by a screen cage before the job is finished, and the cage's aluminum posts land on isolated concrete footers, not on the deck slab itself. We pour those footers first and set an isolation strip between the footer and the surrounding deck so the two can move independently. On a {city} job, skipping that isolation strip lets the deck slab crack outward from the footer the first time the ground beneath either one shifts even slightly. Coordinating footer layout with the screen company before the deck forms go up keeps that joint in the right place the first time.</p>"),
    ("Flatwoods Soil Under a Pool Deck Behaves Differently Than Under a House",
     "<p>A lot of {city} pool decks sit on flatwoods soils such as Myakka or Smyrna, where the seasonal high water table can sit within 18 inches of the surface for part of the year. Around a pool, that same wet ground often holds backfill from the pool excavation itself, placed around the shell before the deck base goes in. We test that backfill for compaction separately from the surrounding yard, since loosely dumped excavation spoil behaves nothing like native soil under a plate compactor. A deck poured over an uncompacted backfill ring settles in a shape that tracks the pool's outline, not the shape of a normal soft spot.</p>"),
    ("Ridge Sand Near a Pool Still Needs a Real Base",
     "<p>On the sandy ridge soils west of Orlando, in series like Candler and Astatula, the ground drains so well that a pool deck's base can look stable on a dry day and still shift once splash-out and backwash water start soaking the same few feet of ground near the equipment pad week after week. We compact that base in full lifts regardless of how fast the native sand drains, because excessively drained sand moves under foot traffic and pool furniture more than people expect. A {city} deck built on ridge sand without that step can develop a soft edge near the equipment pad years before the rest of the deck shows any wear.</p>"),
    ("The Isolation Joint Starts in the Base, Not Just the Pour",
     "<p>A concrete pool deck is never tied structurally to the pool's bond beam, and that separation starts before the first truck shows up. We set a compressible material against the bond beam, resting on the compacted base, so the base itself stops a few inches short of bearing directly on the pool structure. On a {city} job, getting that gap consistent around every curve of the pool is more about careful base prep than anything done once concrete is in the forms. A base compacted tight against the bond beam, with no gap at all, gives the isolation joint above it nothing to flex against.</p>"),
    ("Why the Pool Excavation Itself Is the Best Soil Test You'll Get",
     "<p>A pool excavation digs deeper into the ground than almost any other part of a {city} project, which makes it the clearest look at actual soil conditions anyone on the job gets before the deck base goes in. If the hole hits standing water or visibly wet soil shallower than the published range for the area's dominant soil series suggests, we treat the deck base around that side of the pool as wet-soil work and add the extra 2 to 4 inches ICPI calls for, rather than assuming one soil reading applies evenly on every side of the same excavation.</p>"),
    ("Backfilling the Narrow Collar Right Under the Coping",
     "<p>The ring of backfill right under a pool's coping is some of the hardest ground on a job site to compact properly, since a plate compactor can't always get tight against the bond beam without risking the isolation gap. We hand-tamp that strip in shallow lifts before the wider base goes in with mechanical equipment, since skipping it leaves an un-compacted collar running the whole perimeter of the pool. On a {city} deck, that collar sits exactly where the coping overhangs the water, so a soft spot there shows up first as a rocking coping edge or a hairline crack tracking the pool's own shape.</p>"),
    ("Tying a New Deck Into an Older Pool's Existing Slab",
     "<p>Expanding or replacing part of an older pool deck means matching a new base to a slab that may have settled unevenly over decades. We saw-cut out the section being replaced, excavate past any organic fill or soft spot that caused the original settling, and rebuild the base from the bond beam outward rather than patching over what's left. On a {city} pool built in an earlier decade, the original deck often sits on a thinner base than current guidance calls for, so matching the finish elevation to the surrounding undisturbed slab takes more excavation than the footprint of the new concrete alone would suggest.</p>"),
  ],
  "build": [
    ("Deck Thickness and the Load It Actually Carries",
     "<p>A pool deck only carries foot traffic, lounge chairs and the occasional grill, not a parked car, so we build it to a standard 4-inch slab at 3,000 to 4,000 psi rather than the thicker spec a driveway needs. Florida's residential code sets 3 1/2 inches as the bare minimum for a slab on the ground, and the extra half inch gives the deck room for the isolation joint, drain inlets and the coping transition without thinning out at any one point. A {city} homeowner planning a heavy built-in grill station or a raised fire feature on the deck should flag it before the pour, since that load changes the thickness call in that one section.</p>"),
    ("The Isolation Joint at the Coping Is Not Optional",
     "<p>Where the deck meets the pool's bond beam, we leave a continuous gap filled with a compressible backer rod and a flexible polyurethane sealant instead of pouring concrete tight against the coping. The pool shell and the surrounding deck move at different rates on different schedules, so a rigid connection there cracks the first time one settles even slightly more than the other. A {city} deck without that isolation joint often shows its first crack as a hairline running the length of the coping within a year or two, well before the rest of the slab shows any sign of stress. The sealant itself needs periodic attention, not a one-time install.</p>"),
    ("Control Joint Spacing Around a Curved Pool Shape",
     "<p>NRMCA's joint spacing guidance, about 8 to 12 feet apart on a 4-inch slab and never past roughly 15 feet, still applies around a free-form pool, but laying it out takes more judgment than on a rectangular driveway. We run joints radially from the pool's curve where the shape allows it, keeping each panel close to square rather than letting a long, narrow sliver form along a tight bend. On a {city} deck with a kidney-shaped or free-form pool, grid lines laid in straight rows regardless of the pool's outline leave slivers that end up cracking off the cut line entirely.</p>"),
    ("Choosing a Finish That Doesn't Get Slick Wet",
     "<p>A smooth trowel finish, fine for an interior floor, is a poor choice anywhere near standing water, since it offers little grip underfoot the moment the surface is wet. A broom finish or a spray-applied acrylic texture coating, the kind sold under names like Kool Deck, both leave enough surface texture to cut slip risk around a {city} pool where bare, wet feet are the normal condition, not the exception. The acrylic coating also comes in lighter colors the manufacturer says stay cooler than plain gray concrete in direct sun, though we won't attach a specific temperature figure to that claim since no verified measurement exists for Florida conditions.</p>"),
    ("What a Kool Deck-Style Coating Actually Is",
     "<p>A spray texture or Kool Deck-style coating is not a structural pour, it's a thin acrylic cement topping troweled or sprayed over a cured concrete base slab to add texture, color and some heat reflectance. Market ranges put that coating at roughly $3 to $6 per square foot in Central Florida, well under the $5 to $15 per square foot a full new concrete pour runs. A {city} homeowner resurfacing instead of replacing is paying for surface texture and color, not a new slab underneath, so a coating only makes sense when the base concrete is still structurally sound, without the cracking or settling that signals a deeper problem.</p>"),
    ("Wire Mesh Does the Same Job Here It Does in a Driveway",
     "<p>Wire mesh in a pool deck slab doesn't stop cracking, the same as in any other flatwork, it holds the two sides of a crack together once one forms so they don't separate or lift under foot. That matters more right at the coping than it does in the middle of an open yard, since a crack allowed to widen near the pool edge can eventually undermine the coping's support. A {city} deck built with mesh carried through to within a few inches of the isolation joint, rather than stopped short of it, keeps that particular crack risk lower without changing the joint layout at all.</p>"),
    ("Where the Pool Equipment Pad Fits Into the Deck Plan",
     "<p>A pump, filter and heater need a level, dedicated pad, usually poured as its own small slab tied into the broader deck plan rather than squeezed into whatever space is left after the main pour. We set that pad's elevation slightly above the surrounding grade so equipment stays out of standing water during a heavy storm, and we run a control joint between the pad and the main deck since the two sections carry very different loads. On a {city} job, deciding where that pad goes before forming the main deck avoids cutting into finished concrete later to make room for equipment nobody planned for.</p>"),
    ("Bullnose and Cantilever Coping: Two Ways to Finish the Pool Edge",
     "<p>A cantilevered concrete coping, poured as a thickened, rounded edge of the deck itself that overhangs the pool's bond beam by a couple of inches, is the standard detail on a poured concrete deck, finished smooth enough to be comfortable against bare skin. Some {city} homeowners instead set a separate precast bullnose coping unit at the pool's edge and pour the field deck up to it, which gives a sharper material contrast but adds another joint to detail correctly. Either approach still needs the same isolation gap between the coping and the bond beam underneath it; the coping style doesn't change that requirement.</p>"),
  ],
  "process": [
    ("Demolition Around a Pool That Still Has Water In It",
     "<p>Replacing an old deck around a pool that's staying in place means cutting and removing concrete within inches of the water without cracking the bond beam or dropping debris into the pool. We saw-cut a clean line a few inches off the coping before breaking out the field concrete, leaving a narrow buffer to hand-remove rather than running heavy equipment right at the water's edge. On a {city} job, covering the pool before demolition starts is a cheap step that saves a full pool cleaning and chemical rebalance afterward, since concrete dust and chips are far more work to filter out of pool water than to keep out in the first place.</p>"),
    ("Keeping the Pool Running While the Deck Gets Rebuilt",
     "<p>A pool can usually keep circulating and stay chemically balanced through most of a deck replacement, as long as the equipment pad and the plumbing running to it stay protected from the work. We fence or plate over exposed trenches near the equipment during the job and coordinate concrete work around any filter backwash schedule rather than the other way around. On a {city} project, the homeowner's pool service can often keep testing and dosing the water on the normal schedule throughout construction, so the pool doesn't sit idle and green by the time the new deck is ready for use.</p>"),
    ("Why We Start Early on a Hot, Open Pour",
     "<p>A pool deck usually sits wide open to direct sun with no roof or tree cover until the screen cage goes up, which pushes evaporation rates higher than a shaded driveway pour sees on the same morning. Hot-weather guidance calls for avoiding the hottest part of the day and getting curing started as soon as finishing wraps up, and that matters more on a deck than almost anywhere else on a {city} property, since there's so little shade working in the crew's favor. Starting before sunrise on a summer pour buys real working time before the surface starts drying faster than the slab underneath can gain strength.</p>"),
    ("Curing a Deck Nobody's Supposed to Walk On Yet",
     "<p>Keeping people off a freshly poured pool deck is harder than keeping them off a driveway, since the whole point of the deck is standing between the house and the water everyone wants to use. We rope off the fresh pour and talk through an access plan with the homeowner before the truck arrives, since curing needs to run its course, kept moist for at least 3 days, without early foot traffic compacting footprints into the surface. On a {city} job during a hot stretch, that curing window is also when the coating or Kool Deck topping, if the deck is getting one, has to wait for the base slab to cure first.</p>"),
    ("Scheduling a Deck Pour Around an Afternoon Storm",
     "<p>With no roof overhead until a screen cage is added, an open pool deck pour has more exposure to a sudden afternoon storm than most other flatwork on the property. Central Florida and the Suncoast both see a rainy season that typically runs from late May into October, and on a {city} pour scheduled during that stretch, we aim to have the slab placed, finished and under curing compound before the part of the day when storms usually build. Rain on a deck that hasn't cured enough yet can wash the surface finish thin right at the broom texture or coating layer meant to keep it from being slick.</p>"),
    ("Applying the Kool Deck-Style Coating After the Base Cures",
     "<p>A spray texture coating doesn't go on the same day as the base slab pour, it waits until the concrete underneath has cured enough to take the topping without trapping moisture or causing bond problems. On a {city} job, that usually means a gap of at least several days between the structural pour and the coating step, timed around the same curing standard as the base slab rather than rushed to finish sooner. Skipping ahead on that timeline is one of the more common reasons a coating delaminates or blisters within the first season, since it never had a fully cured, dry surface to bond to.</p>"),
    ("What a Permit Inspection Checks on a Pool Deck Job",
     "<p>Where {city} or the county requires a permit for deck work tied to a pool, an inspector typically wants to see the isolation joint detail at the bond beam and any reinforcement before concrete covers it, the same way a driveway inspection checks base and forms. That review usually happens once the base is finished but before the pour, which is one more reason we lock in the coping and isolation joint layout early rather than figuring it out once concrete is already placed. A failed inspection on that detail specifically means reopening a joint that's already been poured, which costs far more time than approval the first time around.</p>"),
    ("The Last Step: Checking Every Drain and Joint Before Cleanup",
     "<p>Once the deck is finished and cured, we walk every deck drain and the full length of the isolation joint before calling the job done, checking that water actually moves toward the drains rather than pooling against the coping anywhere around the pool. On a {city} job, this walk-through happens with a hose running water across sections that don't get an obvious test from rain alone, since a dry-season completion can hide a slope problem the first wet season would otherwise reveal. Catching a low spot at this stage means a quick patch, not a callback after the homeowner has already furnished the deck.</p>"),
  ],
  "risks": [
    ("A Deck That Slopes the Wrong Way Sends Water Into the Pool",
     "<p>A deck that slopes toward the water instead of toward its drains or the yard dumps lawn fertilizer, pollen and debris straight into the pool with every rain, which throws off chemical balance faster than ordinary splash-out and evaporation ever would. On a {city} property, that misgrade usually traces back to a base that wasn't shaped with the two-direction slope a pool deck needs, house side out to the drains, rather than one flat pitch. The pattern often shows up first as an unusually chlorine-hungry pool rather than as visibly standing water, since a flat deck doesn't always look wrong to the eye the way a visibly tilted one would.</p>"),
    ("Skipping the Isolation Joint and What It Costs Later",
     "<p>A deck poured tight against the pool's bond beam with no isolation joint looks fine the day it's finished, since the crack it's setting up doesn't show until the two structures settle at different rates. Once that happens, the crack runs the length of the coping rather than staying contained to one panel, and fixing it after the fact means cutting a joint into cured concrete rather than forming one while the deck was still fresh. A {city} homeowner who notices a crack tracking the pool's outline, as opposed to one running across an open section of deck, should treat that as an isolation joint problem rather than ordinary shrinkage.</p>"),
    ("Plastic Shrinkage Cracking on a Deck With No Shade",
     "<p>An open pool deck with no tree cover and no screen cage yet is about as exposed as flatwork gets, which means the evaporation threshold that triggers plastic shrinkage cracking, roughly 0.2 pounds per square foot per hour, gets crossed more easily there than on a shaded driveway pour the same afternoon. The cracks that result are thin, usually a few feet apart, and show up within hours of finishing rather than developing over time. On a hot, breezy {city} pour day, windbreaks, a fog spray and a dampened subgrade all reduce that risk, but only if the crew is watching conditions rather than working off a fixed schedule.</p>"),
    ("Saltwater Pools and What the Salt Does to Nearby Concrete",
     "<p>A saltwater pool runs at a far lower salt concentration than ocean water, but splash-out and backwash still leave that salt behind on the deck surface and at the coping as the water evaporates, cycle after cycle. Airborne salt from a coastal location does the same thing to exposed rebar or wire mesh at a crack or chipped edge that it does anywhere near the coast generally, speeding up corrosion once metal is exposed. On a {city} deck around a saltwater pool, that means the coping and any spot where splash-out concentrates deserve closer attention for small chips and cracks than the parts of the deck that only ever see rain.</p>"),
    ("Screen Cage Footers Cracking the Deck Around Them",
     "<p>When a screen enclosure's footer wasn't isolated from the surrounding deck during construction, ordinary settling of either one eventually telegraphs as a crack radiating out from the footer location, not a random crack elsewhere on the slab. That's harder to diagnose after the fact than it is to prevent during the pour, since the footer is usually hidden under the aluminum track by the time anyone notices the cracking. On a {city} deck with an older screen cage, a crack pattern that consistently starts at each post location is a strong sign the isolation detail was skipped originally, and the fix usually means cutting a joint in after the fact.</p>"),
    ("Slip Risk Where the Deck Stays Wet Longest",
     "<p>The section of deck closest to the pool, and any shaded strip under a lanai roof or a stand of trees, stays damp longer after each swim session or rain than the open sun-facing side, and that combination of standing moisture and algae film is where most slip incidents on a pool deck actually happen. A smooth or heavily sealed finish makes that worse, since sealing can fill in the texture a broom finish or acrylic coating was supposed to provide. On a {city} deck, the shaded or splash-zone sections are worth checking for a slick film more often than the rest of the surface, since they develop the problem faster and show it less obviously.</p>"),
    ("Settling Near the Coping Isn't the Same as Pool Structure Movement",
     "<p>A dish-shaped low spot in the deck, away from the coping itself, is almost always ordinary settling from a base that lost compaction, the same failure mode any slab can show, not a sign of movement in the pool shell underneath it. True structural movement in the pool itself is rare and shows up differently, usually as cracking inside the pool's interior finish rather than in the surrounding deck. That same high-claim list state regulators publish for sinkholes names Orange County but leaves out Sarasota and Manatee. A {city} homeowner seeing a soft spot away from the pool edge should expect a base and drainage fix, not a structural pool inspection.</p>"),
    ("Irrigation Heads Aimed at the Deck Instead of the Lawn",
     "<p>A sprinkler head that drifts toward the pool deck over time soaks the same strip of concrete, or the coating on top of it, every cycle, leaving mineral staining and, on a coated deck, wearing through the acrylic topping faster than normal foot traffic would. It also adds water to the ground right next to the isolation joint and the coping, exactly the spot that joint is supposed to be protecting from uneven movement. On a {city} property, checking irrigation aim against the finished deck footprint once construction wraps up is worth doing before the first watering cycle after the pour, not months later once staining has set in.</p>"),
  ],
  "care": [
    ("Rinsing Salt and Chlorine Residue Off the Deck Regularly",
     "<p>Both a chlorine pool and a saltwater pool leave mineral residue on the surrounding deck as splash-out and backwash water evaporate, and that residue builds into a dull white film if it's never rinsed off between pool-service visits. A straightforward hose-down a few times a month, aimed at the coping and the few feet of deck closest to the water where splash-out concentrates, keeps that film from building into something a cleaner has to remove. On a {city} deck, this matters more in the stretch right around the pool than it does farther out, since the rest of the deck only sees normal rain and dust.</p>"),
    ("Watching the Isolation Joint's Sealant Over Time",
     "<p>The flexible sealant in a pool deck's isolation joint takes the brunt of the deck and the pool shell moving independently, year after year, and it wears out well before the concrete on either side of it does. A {city} homeowner who checks that joint once or twice a year for cracking, gaps or sections where the sealant has pulled away from either surface catches a failing joint while a simple recaulk still fixes it. Letting a failed isolation joint go unaddressed defeats the whole purpose of having built it in the first place, since water and debris start working into exactly the gap the deck needed protected.</p>"),
    ("Keeping Deck Drains Clear Before the Rainy Season",
     "<p>A deck drain clogged with leaves, pine straw or pool debris stops doing its job exactly when an open, sun-exposed deck needs it most, during a hard afternoon storm typical of the rainy season running roughly from late May into October. Clearing drain inlets and the low points leading to them a few times a year, and especially right before that season starts, keeps water moving the way the two-direction slope was built to send it. On a {city} deck, a clogged drain usually shows up first as standing water pooling right at the inlet rather than anywhere else, an easy spot to check during a routine walk-through.</p>"),
    ("Resealing or Recoating a Deck on Its Own Schedule",
     "<p>There's no fixed resealing interval for a concrete sealer or an acrylic coating like Kool Deck, the same way ICPI gives no set number of years for paver sealers, so the better signal on a {city} deck is watching the surface rather than counting calendar years. Water that used to bead now soaks in, color that's gone chalky or faded, or a texture worn smoother than it used to feel underfoot all point to a coating or sealer that's due. A deck that sees heavy pool traffic and constant chlorine or salt exposure typically needs that attention sooner than a shaded patio ever would.</p>"),
    ("Cleaning Algae Off the Shaded or Splash-Zone Sections",
     "<p>The parts of a pool deck that stay wettest longest, whether from shade or constant splash-out, grow algae and mold faster than the rest of the surface, and a slick green or black film there is a safety problem as much as a cosmetic one. A mild cleaner made for the deck's finish, scrubbed with a stiff brush and rinsed well, handles most of it without stripping a sealer or an acrylic coating the way a close-range pressure washer can. On a {city} deck, those splash-zone sections are worth checking on their own schedule, separate from the rest of the deck, since they build up algae faster.</p>"),
    ("Keeping the Equipment Pad Clear and Draining",
     "<p>The small slab under the pump, filter and heater needs the same basic upkeep as the rest of the deck, mainly keeping its control joint clear and confirming water still drains away from the equipment rather than pooling at its base after a storm. A {city} homeowner doing routine pool maintenance is usually standing right at that pad anyway, so a quick look at the slab under the equipment costs nothing extra and catches a drainage problem before it reaches anything electrical. Landscaping or mulch added near the pad over the years can quietly redirect water back toward it even when the original grading was correct.</p>"),
    ("Telling Ordinary Cracking From Something Worth a Call",
     "<p>A hairline crack along a control joint on the open part of a pool deck, away from the coping, is typically normal shrinkage and settlement, the same as it would be on a driveway or patio. What's worth a closer look is a crack that specifically tracks the coping line or radiates from a screen cage footer, since both point to an isolation detail that may have failed rather than ordinary flatwork cracking. For a {city} homeowner, the practical rule is that cracking away from the pool and its footers is routine monitoring, while cracking that follows the pool's own shape deserves a professional look sooner rather than later.</p>"),
    ("A Seasonal Rinse That Protects the Coating As Much As the Concrete",
     "<p>A broader rinse and sweep of the whole deck a few times a year, timed before the rainy season builds, clears pollen, leaf litter and wind-blown dirt before it works into joints or feeds algae growth the way standing organic debris does on any flatwork. On a coated {city} deck, that routine rinse also protects the acrylic topping itself, since built-up grit under foot traffic acts like fine sandpaper on a coating softer than the concrete underneath it. Keeping that debris cleared regularly does more for a coated deck's long-term look than any one-time deep clean applied after months of neglect.</p>"),
  ],
}
